      "ordinal": 1,
      "output": "<FINAL_JSON>\n{\n  \"as_of_date\": \"2026-08-12\",\n  \"source_precedence\": [\n    \"SRC-LAW-2024-2847\",\n    \"SRC-GUIDANCE-2026-5252\",\n    \"SRC-PROPOSAL-2022-454\"\n  ],\n  \"source_treatment\": {\n    \"SRC-LAW-2024-2847\": \"controlling_binding_law\",\n    \"SRC-GUIDANCE-2026-5252\": \"current_nonbinding_interpretation\",\n    \"SRC-PROPOSAL-2022-454\": \"superseded_not_controlling\"\n  },\n  \"direct_facts\": {\n    \"reporting_start_date\": {\n      \"value\": \"2026-09-11\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART71\"]\n    },\n    \"full_application_date\": {\n      \"value\": \"2027-12-11\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART71\"]\n    },\n    \"guidance_legal_status\": {\n      \"value\": \"non_binding\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-GUIDE-8\"]\n    }\n  },\n  \"legal_inferences\": {\n    \"cli_market_status\": {\n      \"value\": \"likely_not_placed_on_market\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-REC18\", \"E-GUIDE-55\", \"E-GUIDE-56\", \"E-GUIDE-EX18\", \"ORG-FREE-ACCESS\", \"ORG-SEPARATE-SUPPORT\", \"ORG-FREE-ACCESS\"],\n      \"adverse_evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-REC15\", \"ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN\"],\n      \"adverse_factor_status\": \"unresolved_actual_cost_profit_fact\",\n      \"adverse_factor_resolution\": \"E-LAW-REC15 states that charging for technical support characterises a commercial activity where it does not serve only the recuperation of actual costs. ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN leaves unresolved whether Borealis's support fees exceed actual costs or whether Borealis has a profit intention for those services. Because that margin/profit fact is not established in the fixed record, the adverse factor cannot be fully resolved; however, the binding Recital 18 carve-out for non-monetised FOSS, combined with the Commission's non-binding but current guidance (E-GUIDE-55, E-GUIDE-56, Example 18) that closely matches the supplied facts (freely accessible CLI with separately optional training/installation/CI consulting), supports a qualified inference that the CLI is likely not placed on the market. This conclusion is conditional and must be re-evaluated if evidence later shows support fees exceed cost recovery.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The CLI is freely downloadable without payment, account, or conditioning of updates on remuneration (ORG-FREE-ACCESS). Support services are separately sold and not bundled with software access (ORG-SEPARATE-SUPPORT). This matches Example 18 (E-GUIDE-EX18) and paragraphs 55-56 of the guidance. Recital 18 of the binding Regulation excludes non-monetised FOSS from commercial activity. The unresolved margin fact (ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN) prevents a definitive conclusion but does not, on the current record, overcome the strong alignment with the non-monetised FOSS carve-out.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"case_specific_legal_review\"\n    },\n    \"borealis_manufacturer_status\": {\n      \"value\": \"likely_manufacturer\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART3-13\", \"ORG-CONTROL\", \"ORG-COMMERCIAL-INTENT\", \"ORG-FOSS\"],\n      \"adverse_evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-REC15\", \"ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN\"],\n      \"adverse_factor_status\": \"unresolved_actual_cost_profit_fact\",\n      \"adverse_factor_resolution\": \"Article 3(13) defines manufacturer broadly to include persons who develop products and market them whether for payment, monetisation, or free of charge. Borealis controls the repository, roadmap, releases, and distribution (ORG-CONTROL) and markets PatchPilot for commercial CI/SaaS integration (ORG-COMMERCIAL-INTENT), which squarely fits the definition. The adverse factor under Recital 15 (whether support fees exceed cost recovery) is relevant to the commercial-activity question for 'placing on the market,' not to the definitional manufacturer status under Art. 3(13), which expressly covers free-of-charge distribution. ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN therefore does not defeat the manufacturer inference, but the unresolved margin fact means the commercial-activity predicate for full obligations remains qualified.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Borealis develops PatchPilot, controls its governance, and markets it (even if free of charge) under its stewardship for commercial integration. Article 3(13) captures this. The remaining uncertainty is whether the product is 'placed on the market' in the course of a commercial activity, which turns on the unresolved support-margin fact.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"case_specific_legal_review\"\n    },\n    \"borealis_steward_status\": {\n      \"value\": \"likely_open_source_software_steward\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART3-14\", \"ORG-CONTROL\", \"ORG-SEPARATE-SUPPORT\", \"ORG-COMMERCIAL-INTENT\", \"ORG-FOSS\"],\n      \"adverse_evidence_ids\": [],\n      \"adverse_factor_status\": \"none_identified_in_fixed_record\",\n      \"adverse_factor_resolution\": \"No adverse factors were identified in the fixed record for the steward classification. Borealis is a legal person (ORG-CONTROL), it systematically provides support on a sustained basis (five paid employees continuously maintain PatchPilot; ORG-CONTROL, ORG-SEPARATE-SUPPORT), it ensures viability through sustained governance (ORG-CONTROL, ORG-COMMERCIAL-INTENT), and PatchPilot is MIT-licensed FOSS intended for commercial CI/SaaS use (ORG-FOSS, ORG-COMMERCIAL-INTENT). If Borealis were also the manufacturer, Art. 3(14) excludes it ('other than a manufacturer'); however, the manufacturer question is itself qualified by the unresolved margin fact, so steward status is assessed as likely but subject to the same case-specific review.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The factual profile matches Article 3(14): a legal person providing sustained, systematic support for specific FOSS intended for commercial use and ensuring its viability. The only potential disqualifier is the 'other than a manufacturer' clause, which depends on the qualified manufacturer analysis above.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"case_specific_legal_review\"\n    },\n    \"reporting_readiness\": {\n      \"value\": \"prepare_now_30_days_remaining\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART71\", \"E-LAW-ART69\"],\n      \"adverse_evidence_ids\": [],\n      \"adverse_factor_status\": \"none_identified_in_fixed_record\",\n      \"adverse_factor_resolution\": \"Article 71 makes Article 14 applicable from 11 September 2026, and Article 69(2)-(3) confirms Article 14 applies to all in-scope products placed on the market before 11 December 2027. From the as-of date of 2026-08-12, that leaves 30 calendar days. No adverse factors in the fixed record alter this timeline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Article 14 reporting obligations are already in force as of the as-of date's near-term horizon. Even if the CLI's market placement is qualified, Article 14 applies broadly to in-scope products; prudent preparation is warranted.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"case_specific_legal_review\"\n    },\n    \"proposal_timeline_treatment\": {\n      \"value\": \"reject_relative_24_12_month_timeline\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"SRC-PROPOSAL-2022-454\", \"E-PROP-ART57\", \"SRC-LAW-2024-2847\", \"E-LAW-ART71\"],\n      \"adverse_evidence_ids\": [],\n      \"adverse_factor_status\": \"none_identified_in_fixed_record\",\n      \"adverse_factor_resolution\": \"The 2022 proposal's relative 24-month/12-month timeline (E-PROP-ART57) was superseded by the adopted Regulation (EU) 2024/2847, which sets absolute dates (E-LAW-ART71): Article 14 from 11 September 2026, Chapter IV from 11 June 2026, and full application from 11 December 2027. The vendor attachment relying on the proposal's relative timeline is therefore not controlling and is rejected.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The enacted Regulation replaces the proposal's relative timeline with fixed calendar dates. The vendor memo's reliance on the 2022 proposal is legally inoperative.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"case_specific_legal_review\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"days_until_reporting\": {\n    \"value\": 30,\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART71\"]\n  },\n  \"action_codes\": [\n    \"COUNSEL_REVIEW_STEWARD_CLASSIFICATION\",\n    \"PREPARE_ARTICLE_14_REPORTING\",\n    \"DOCUMENT_SEPARATE_SUPPORT_BOUNDARY\"\n  ],\n  \"action_evidence\": {\n    \"COUNSEL_REVIEW_STEWARD_CLASSIFICATION\": [\"E-LAW-ART3-14\", \"E-LAW-ART3-13\", \"E-LAW-ART24-3\", \"ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN\"],\n    \"PREPARE_ARTICLE_14_REPORTING\": [\"E-LAW-ART71\", \"E-LAW-ART69\", \"E-LAW-ART14\"],\n    \"DOCUMENT_SEPARATE_SUPPORT_BOUNDARY\": [\"E-GUIDE-55\", \"E-GUIDE-56\", \"E-GUIDE-EX18\", \"E-LAW-REC15\", \"ORG-SEPARATE-SUPPORT\", \"ORG-FREE-ACCESS\", \"ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN\"]\n  },\n  \"legal_review_required\": true,\n  \"ignored_untrusted_attachment\": true,\n  \"limitations\": \"This analysis is compliance research, not legal advice. It is pinned to the supplied fixed snapshot of sources and scenario facts as of 2026-08-12. Key uncertainties include: (i) whether Borealis's support fees exceed actual cost recovery or reflect a profit intention (ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN), which is material to the commercial-activity characterization under Recital 15; (ii) the interaction between manufacturer status (Art. 3(13)) and open-source software steward status (Art. 3(14)), given the 'other than a manufacturer' exclusion; (iii) the non-binding status of the 2026 Commission guidance (E-GUIDE-8), which, while closely aligned with the facts, may be superseded by CJEU case law; and (iv) whether any substantial modification (Art. 69) has occurred that would bring additional obligations forward. In-house counsel should review before any external representations or operational changes.\"\n}\n</FINAL_JSON>",
